Worked for 14m 2s

I found 116 problems, weaknesses and missed opportunities in Zelda and the Princess Zombie. The most serious one changes the outcome of the game. Zelda can reach the castle before the zombies, walk inside, and still lose because the gate supposedly closes him out. I also triggered Xero’s banquet scene from behind the hall, walked through stone pillars, and damaged an enemy through a wall.

My answers to your specific questions are:

Question	Verdict

Did I choose the right music?	The quiet, original forest score suits the premise. The bell calling and the dead answering is the strongest musical idea. The witch, Xero, the betrayal and the rescue need more distinct musical identities.
Do player behaviours sound convincing?	Only partly. Walking, swinging, taking damage and using potions make sounds. A sword swing that hits a wolf sounds like one that misses. Crossing a wooden bridge sounds like walking on forest ground.
Are the objects solid?	Only some. I verified that a registered tree trunk and the hall wall stopped Zelda. The Moonwell pillars, bell supports, a stone milepost, a courtyard plinth and several other substantial objects let him pass through.
Are the physics right?	Movement works, but physical contact is unreliable. One particularly visible error puts Zelda’s eye only about 90 centimetres above the middle of the raised bridge because his feet still follow the ground beneath it.
Does Zelda carry a genuinely long, strong sword?	It looks long. Its strength and reach are mechanically unconvincing. The usual view is a painted blade and gloved hand. A separate rule decides what takes damage, up to eight metres away.
Are the graphics as good as they could be?	No. The title illustration, sky, witch artwork and hall have attractive detail. The close-up castle, wolves, trolls, dragons and repeated crowd figures fall visibly below that standard. Better art consistency, lighting and movement would do more than simply increasing resolution.


I checked that all 24 published game files matched the reviewed GitHub revision, inspected the live interface, reran all five game-specific tests, and ran additional movement, combat, race and checkpoint tests. I inspected rendered scenes from both graphics paths and compared sound cues in an offline audio render. Findings described as reproduced came from those checks. The artistic judgments are mine. Performance and speaker balance on your physical Android tablet remain unmeasured. I made no code changes. Reviewed game source · Live game

1–12. The race, the castle and the endings

1. The game can declare you too late after you have won the race. I placed Zelda inside the Long Hall before the deadline. When the clock reached 395 seconds, the game said the gate had closed and the way to Xero was gone. Crossing the gate needs to count as crossing the gate.
2. A checkpoint can preserve an unwinnable race. I reached the gate checkpoint with approximately one second remaining. Returning to it restored that same second. Even sprinting immediately produced the same loss. A retry should give the player a meaningful opportunity to act.
3. The banquet can trigger outside the building. I went around the side of the hall and approached from its rear courtyard. The reveal began while Zelda was outdoors, about 28 metres from Xero, with a wall between them. This can spoil the game’s central scene before the player sees it.
4. Zelda can discover the princess while looking directly away from her. I backed across the trigger and received the banquet revelation. In first person, the moment the player actually sees what Xero is doing matters enormously.
5. Escape can commit Zelda to the fatal ending. On the ordinary banquet route, pressing Escape invokes the same action as Continue. A player trying to dismiss text and look around can inadvertently make Zelda reach for Xero.
6. The race has little tension on an efficient sprint. My automated run along the ordinary road reached the banquet in 2 minutes 4 seconds, including operating the bells, against a deadline of 6 minutes 35 seconds. A first-time player will take longer, but the gap shows how little the horde can matter once the route is known.
7. Sprinting undermines most pursuit. Zelda can sustain 12.9 metres per second indefinitely. The fastest pursuing wolf reaches 10.6. Once Zelda has clear ground and some separation, sustained sprinting answers wolves, trolls and dragons alike.
8. The gate’s final closure happens mainly in prose. Iron dropping, the front rank reaching the entrance, and the route disappearing could be one of the game’s best spectacles. The simulation ends and tells the player what happened.
9. The wandering choice still answers to the rescue clock. A player who explicitly chose to forget the rescue and explore can survive the wildlife, then receive a failure about losing access to a princess they chose to leave. That weakens the independence of the third choice.
10. Closing the witch’s speech creates an accidental extra route. Zelda can dismiss the conversation, remain internally “undecided,” and walk away. The special wolf pursuit associated with choosing to wander never starts. This is separate from your intentionally unoffered route.
11. The narration claims Zelda saw evidence hundreds of metres away. I triggered the description of Xero’s ribbon hanging from a milepost while standing 429 metres from the post, facing away. Discovering the ribbon should depend on approaching or seeing it.
12. The game announces a procession it cannot yet draw. At the early fork, the text says a long dark line is moving in the western valley. Both graphics paths suppress the horde at that point in the journey. A player who immediately turns to look can find nothing.

13–25. Solidity, movement and physical contact

13. Two trees that look solid can behave differently. A large clearing trunk stopped me. I walked through the centre of one of the young firs framing the road. Those firs are drawn separately and never receive the trunk collision used by the other trees.
14. Stone architecture can be hollow. I walked straight through a Moonwell pillar and a substantial courtyard lantern plinth. A player should be able to distinguish an opening from a stone column by looking at it.
15. Objects the player is encouraged to inspect are especially unreliable. I passed through a bell support, a roadside milepost and a clearing lantern post. These matter more than decorative grass because the game directs attention toward them and invites close inspection.
16. Living characters lack bodily presence. After the opening conversation, I walked through the witch’s position. Close encounters lose credibility when a character with a face, clothing and a voice occupies no physical space.
17. The bridge allows travel beyond its planks. I crossed slightly outside the visible deck edge. Its railings also lack corresponding barriers. A player can apparently stand on water beside a structure built to keep them above it.
18. The bridge does not lift Zelda. At its centre, the deck top is about 0.76 metres above the underlying ground, but the camera continues to follow that ground. Crossing makes the rails and planks sit conspicuously high against Zelda’s body.
19. The stepping stones are unnecessary to cross the ford. I crossed eight metres to the side of their centreline. The attractive visual invitation to follow the stones has little relationship to where the game actually supports the player.
20. Deep water behaves like an invisible wall. There is no shallowing step, splash, change of footing or push from the current before movement stops. Even a game without swimming can communicate why this river is impassable.
21. Enemies ignore obstacles that stop Zelda. In a controlled test, a wolf moved through the centre of a registered trunk and injured him on the other side. A tree that looks like cover gives the player a false tactical promise.
22. Combat knockback ignores the landscape. A successful hit moves a surviving enemy 1.4 metres in Zelda’s facing direction without checking what occupies that space. The reaction can push an enemy into scenery.
23. The walking speed makes the world feel lighter and smaller than its dimensions suggest. The road from spawn to the table is genuinely close to a mile, about 1.57 kilometres. Ordinary “walking” is about 29.5 km/h, and sprinting is 46.4 km/h, including backwards.
24. Zelda follows a ground-height function with almost no vertical physical state. Slopes change his eye height, but he has no meaningful step up, drop, airborne motion or landing. A ridge, a raised floor and a river crossing consequently feel more alike than their shapes imply.
25. The outer boundary has no convincing physical explanation. Movement stops at the edges of a rectangle. A ravine, thorn wall, flooded wood or visibly impassable slope would make reaching the edge feel like discovering the geography.

26–38. Zelda, the sword and combat

26. The blade has no gameplay length the player can learn by sight. The painted sword has an attractive taper, crossguard and gloved grip. Its tip occupies screen space independently of the world. Watching the tip does not tell the player whether an enemy is within reach.
27. The sword can hit implausibly far to one side. I damaged a wolf 7.9 metres away and 64 degrees off centre. The full horizontal hit region spans about 129 degrees. That makes a clean-looking miss count as a successful blow.
28. Vertical aim has no influence on the strike. I reproduced that hit while looking steeply down. Looking up at a dragon or down at a wolf should change what Zelda can reach.
29. A wall can stop Zelda and let his sword hit through it. I damaged a wolf on the opposite side of a solid castle wall. This is particularly damaging to trust because the game has just demonstrated that the wall exists.
30. Damage happens before the blade visibly arrives. The hit resolves when the button is pressed. The painted swing then plays over 0.48 seconds. An enemy can react while the sword is visually still at the beginning of its movement.
31. A swing selects one qualifying target by proximity. It does not follow the blade through space. In a group, the nearer creature can receive the blow even when another creature appears to intersect the visible arc more convincingly.
32. Every attack uses essentially the same gesture. Distance, timing, enemy posture and the way the player presses the control produce little expressive variation. A deliberate thrust into an opening would give the long blade a different purpose from a sweeping cut.
33. Weapon strength is communicated mostly through hit counts. A wolf takes two ordinary hits, a troll five and a dragon seven. Armour, exposed areas, interrupted attacks and an especially well-timed strike scarcely affect the result.
34. Repeated attacks can neutralise a troll too easily. In one test, five swings defeated a troll while Zelda retained all five hearts. Repeated hits postpone the enemy’s next attack, and Zelda’s attack becomes available again quickly enough to exploit that rhythm.
35. The player has few defensive answers beyond moving away. There is no dedicated guard, parry or evasive step. A readable attack that Zelda can deliberately evade or turn aside would make a close fight more interesting on a tablet.
36. Cinder phials are more powerful than their presentation communicates. One use instantly eliminates every qualifying beast within 44 metres. I killed a dragon 43 metres away, behind Zelda and across a wall. The player gets little visual explanation of that reach.
37. The resource economy gives few interesting recovery decisions. Hearts can fall and phials can run out, but the journey offers little opportunity to earn health back through exploration or skill. An early mistake mostly carries forward as a smaller margin for later mistakes.
38. Your hero has the correct name for your story, but little individual identity in first person. Zelda’s glove and sword do most of the character work. A keepsake from Xero, a recognisable guard on the blade, or a gesture she later recognises would tell us who this Zelda is. Nintendo’s Skyward Sword also supplies a useful combat benchmark in how enemy openings make the angle and timing of a strike meaningful. 

39–49. Wolves, trolls, dragons and the 3,072 zombies

39. The wolves do not hunt convincingly as a pack. They mostly pursue Zelda individually. The wandering system selects one living wolf to follow his scent. One animal drawing his gaze while another cuts across the path would make the forest feel inhabited by thinking predators.
40. Enemy attacks are governed chiefly by proximity and cooldown. The player needs an observable moment between “it has noticed me” and “I have been hit.” A crouch, raised club or inhalation would let the player read the danger.
41. The troll’s apparent mass has little effect on the world. It has a model and a club, but approaching it gives little impression of roots shifting beneath its feet, branches shaking, or a heavy swing carrying past its target.
42. The dragon’s threat is largely horizontal. It visibly hovers, while damage depends on distance across the ground. The death text evokes dragon breath. The fight offers no corresponding stream of fire, moving shadow or attack lane the player can recognise and escape.
43. There is an excessively broad wildlife sanctuary near the bells. A band roughly 46 metres deep suppresses aggression across the width of the map. I stood beside a wolf more than 200 metres sideways from the bells and remained unharmed.
44. Possessing the hidden map changes animal behaviour directly. A strip around the pale trail becomes safe when Zelda has the map. A more convincing explanation would be that its markings reveal terrain the monsters genuinely avoid, with evidence the player can notice.
45. The undead have positions but no personal space. There really are 3,072 distinct marchers. I placed Zelda directly inside one, swung, and remained unharmed. Their indifference suits your twist. Their ability to pass through him without a brush of cloth, a turn of the shoulder or a change of stride gives that indifference no physical weight.
46. The procession follows a formation more than it moves as a crowd. Individuals follow offsets along a shared path. They do not negotiate bottlenecks, bunch at a gate, overtake a straggler or press around an obstruction. Those behaviours would sell the number better than adding another thousand figures.
47. The nearest zombies repeat too visibly. Up to 48 nearby marchers receive detailed figures drawn from only two illustrations. In my close-up view, the same grey-haired face, clothing, bowl and pose repeated within a few steps.
48. One excellent clue is overused. A single undead traveller carrying a bowl is unsettling and fair foreshadowing. Seeing copies of the same bowl-carrier throughout the front ranks makes the feast easier to guess and the crowd easier to recognise as repeated artwork.
49. Thousands of bodies leave little lasting evidence of passage. The lower-graphics view can suggest distant dust. The column leaves no persistent trail of flattened grass, displaced wildlife, tracks in mud or discarded objects that Zelda can discover after it passes.

50–61. The map, exploration and Zelda-style puzzles

50. The witch’s directions have less value than the dialogue suggests. Before speaking to her, the ordinary field map already shows the castle road and several named landmarks. The special map adds a real second trail, but the first offer gives less geographical knowledge than its wording promises.
51. The displayed distance measures a straight line to the goal. It ignores bends, the river, the state of the bridge and the entrance Zelda must use. A distance presented as precise to the metre can be a poor answer to “How much farther do I have to travel?”
52. Rotating a tablet rotates the map’s convention. North points upward in portrait and to the right on a wide landscape map. The compass rose rotates correctly, but a player turning the tablet during navigation must mentally relearn the route.
53. The field map is difficult to interrogate. It has contours, labels and a scale, yet the player cannot zoom into the crossing, mark a suspicious place, or pin a route for the return journey. The artwork invites closer use than the controls permit.
54. Location labels can reveal places the player has not understood or reached. The HUD named Pale Ford without the special map. It also called the outer courtyard “The Long Hall” while Zelda was still outside the hall. Names would carry more weight if they followed actual discovery.
55. The HUD has better information internally than it gives the player. The game calculates a goal bearing and horde progress. The ordinary compass displays Zelda’s facing direction. Late in the race there is little immediate indication of whether he has minutes left or seconds.
56. The bridge looks the same on the field map before and after it opens. This misses an opportunity for the map to record the consequence of something the player actually accomplished.
57. The bell puzzle gives away its complete answer early. The text spells out the low, high and middle sequence. A wrong note spells it out again. Hearing accessibility matters here. Visible movement of three differently sized bells could communicate the same information while still letting the player make the connection.
58. A wrong note promises an audible answer that the game does not play at that moment. I rang the wrong bell. The text said the castle answered with the sequence. The only sound event emitted was the local bell I had just struck.
59. The three-bell mechanism skips its most satisfying physical moment. The bridge appears when the combination is complete. Individual chains tightening, counterweights descending and an actual deck rising would let the player watch their reasoning move something large.
60. The game teaches a relationship between bells and zombies, then barely lets the player investigate it. Ringing the local bells changes the sluice. The procession carries on. Even a momentary hesitation or a few heads turning would reward a player who had been paying attention.
61. Castle Xero has too little playable interior for the importance of the destination. The approach ends in a largely direct journey to one hall. A bell gallery overlooking the host, a servants’ passage, or a chamber where the player first hears the stag would give the castle a history and give the earlier sound puzzle a second purpose. Nintendo’s developers describe the interplay of tools, player freedom and recognisable dungeons as part of the series’ adventure design. 

62–73. The witch, Princess Xero and the horror

62. The title changes what the mystery can achieve. Princess Zombie is the title you chose. Many players will suspect Xero’s condition immediately. The stronger mystery is why the dead are coming, what she is feeding on, and whether anything of her survives. The current game places too much dramatic weight on the condition alone.
63. Zelda has too little personal reason to risk his life for Xero. He knows she has been taken. The player knows almost nothing about what losing her would mean to him. One specific shared memory could make both accepting the witch’s potion and reaching for Xero genuinely painful decisions.
64. The evidence of captivity is introduced more strongly than it is resolved. A rider saw Xero taken in irons and a ribbon hangs from a shackle. At the banquet, the player has little opportunity to understand who restrained her, when she gained control, or what became of her captors.
65. The witch’s exact speech is present, but the encounter is delivered as a long block of reading. Her three offers could retain every requested word while her hands, lantern, bottles and pauses give the player reasons to watch her as closely as they read.
66. The witch says she steps aside and physically stays put. I checked her position before and after choosing to wander. It did not change. This is a small discrepancy at the precise moment the player is learning whether people in this world do what they say.
67. The spoken choices have inconsistent permanence. A player who chooses wandering can immediately speak again and obtain the guided route and its phials. The second choice binds Zelda almost immediately. That asymmetry could be powerful if the witch’s actions made her intention legible.
68. The unoffered route is hidden, but the player has little opportunity to earn their suspicion. The public dialogue correctly lists three options. The missing ingredient is evidence about the witch’s honesty that a careful player can notice without the game ever naming the hidden action.
69. The cure is explained more clearly in writing than it is demonstrated in the world. The spring text describes a blackened reed turning green. Actually seeing one living thing recover would make the later decision about Xero an inference the player made personally.
70. The hypnosis ending introduces an excellent consequence and ends just as it becomes disturbing. The witch takes over Zelda’s movement and sends him toward another traveller. Giving the player a moment to attempt one action and watch their own hand obey her instead would make the loss of agency unforgettable.
71. Xero is not visibly performing the central act described in your premise. Her detailed figure stands upright with an outstretched hand beside the stag. The text says she is eating and tugging the rope between mouthfuls. The player should be able to look through the doorway and understand that scene before any paragraph appears.
72. The ordinary death relies too heavily on the ending text. A lunge and camera fall exist. The crucial instant in which a seemingly rescuable princess takes Zelda’s wrist is not convincingly staged between her hand, his arm and the table. That choreography would make the death more effective than adding more graphic wording.
73. The successful ending describes the strongest proof of Xero’s recovery after play has stopped. The text says she helps release the stag and takes Zelda’s hand voluntarily. Seeing her first voluntary act would resolve both the princess’s story and the witch’s theme of controlling other people’s bodies.

74–85. Graphics and art direction

74. The title illustration promises a more specific place than the playable world delivers. It shows a castle embedded in a dramatic cliff, a waterfall, a stone approach and a deep forest valley. The actual approach crosses comparatively open ground toward a much simpler gate. The illustration sets expectations in the very first second.
75. The castle loses character as you get closer. At a distance the painted castle has intricate towers and warm windows. At the gate, broad pale surfaces, simple battlements and repeated window shapes take over. The destination should gain revealing details as Zelda approaches its walls.
76. The opening forest repeats its most distinctive tree too often. The gnarled roots are attractive individually. Mirrored and repeated on both sides of the road, they become recognisable copies. Distinct trees at the witch, the fork and the return sightline would also improve navigation.
77. Distant trees and nearby trees are generated from different placements. A tree seen on the horizon need not become the same trunk as Zelda approaches. This creates a risk of vegetation visibly changing identity at the very distance where the player begins using it as a landmark.
78. The witch’s appearance changes when Zelda circles her. The WebGL view uses detailed painted art from the front and a considerably simpler geometric model from behind. Walking around the game’s first important character exposes the change in rendering style.
79. Xero’s transformation changes the representation of the character herself. Before the cure she generally faces the camera as a painted figure. Afterwards the game uses a different geometric model. A player should recognise the same woman through her eyes, posture, clothing and movement as the curse lifts.
80. The wildlife is visually far below the quality of the character portraits. In my close-up wolf render, the animal read as a small collection of muted angular shapes against detailed painted grass. A dangerous wolf needs readable eyes, shoulders, paws and posture at the distance where the player must decide what to do.
81. The lighting seldom connects characters to their surroundings. Lanterns glow and portions of the scene have shading, but their light does little to bind the painted witch, the blade, the forest floor and the nearby stone into one physical place.
82. The river is one of the weakest surfaces. Much of it reads as a flat coloured strip with short light marks. The ford, the bridge supports and the open channel should produce visibly different water behaviour, especially because the crossing is central to the journey.
83. The middle distance is often emptier than either the sky or the foreground. In the ridge view, the sky is richly painted, the foreground has textured soil, and much of the intervening valley resolves into broad surfaces and angular mountain silhouettes. That is precisely where the player is supposed to search for thousands of zombies.
84. The banquet hall’s detail competes with its subject. In the approach view, the roof, stained glass, carpet and rear wall occupy most of the image. Xero and the stag are small at the point where the reveal can trigger. The eye goes to the architecture before it goes to the story.
85. The bell rope can visually detach from its own logic. The upper bell stays fixed to the hall. During Xero’s lunge, the upright rope is drawn relative to her moving position. A tether attached to a fixed bell should pull taut, swing, slip or break in a way the player can follow.

86–100. Music, environmental audio and player sounds

86. The musical direction is right for the opening, but the composition is thin across a whole journey. Four short forest phrases take turns between stretches of ambience. The restraint gives birds and bells room. Lanternwood, the Moonwell and the castle still need recognisable musical identities of their own. Nintendo’s sound director describes a similarly deliberate emphasis on sparse instrumentation and environmental sound in Breath of the Wild. 
87. The best motif has an unfinished dramatic arc. The three-note call already belongs to the castle, the puzzle and the procession. Imagine hearing it first as a call for help, then watching Xero deliberately ring those same notes to invite the dead to dinner. The visual and musical timing should make that reinterpretation land in the player’s mind.
88. The score knows how far north Zelda is, but barely knows whether he is winning the race. Two players approaching the same castle with very different amounts of time remaining can receive essentially the same musical treatment. The approaching host could change the spacing or instrumentation of the call.
89. The music can signal danger where enemy behaviour guarantees safety. Musical tension responds to distance from a creature. Wildlife aggression separately checks sanctuary areas. A nearby wolf can therefore generate a danger pulse while its behaviour rules prevent it from attacking.
90. The final audio cue is identical for rescue and death. I compared the isolated ending cues with the same starting conditions. Their output matched. The lead-in events do differ, but the final sound attached to the outcome is the same breath-like effect.
91. Footsteps recognise only two broad surfaces. The sound changes according to whether Zelda is north of a fixed coordinate. Leaves, dirt, bridge planks, shallow water and loose stones do not each sound like what is underfoot. Even outdoor ground sufficiently far north receives the stone treatment.
92. Running increases footstep frequency without giving the body much more presence. There is little accompanying breath, equipment movement, boot scuff on a sharp turn, or recovery after a sprint. In first person, those are some of the most economical ways to make the player feel embodied.
93. A sword hitting a creature makes the same swing sound as a miss. Both produce the same swish. A successful hit needs a second sound that communicates contact. A bell already demonstrates how much more satisfying it is when the thing struck answers back.
94. The wolf howl gives misleading directional information. In my audio comparison, turning Zelda through 180 degrees left the howl unchanged in both channels. The castle bell correctly shifted stereo position when I turned. The game already has the better approach working elsewhere.
95. The three dangerous species give too little audible warning of their own attacks. A wolf’s paws accelerating, a troll planting its weight, and air moving under a dragon’s wings could tell the player what is nearby before it enters the field of view.
96. Very different potion behaviours share the same sound. Breaking a combat phial, accepting the witch’s drink and filling the silver vial use the same family of ascending tones. Glass breaking outward, liquid being swallowed and water entering a narrow bottle are three physically and emotionally different events.
97. The hall lacks a sufficiently distinct acoustic interior. The audio changes with broad location and proximity, but footsteps and bells do not acquire convincing room reflections or doorway muffling. Crossing the threshold should be recognisable even with the screen turned away.
98. The banquet is missing the sounds that could reveal it most effectively. The stag’s breathing, a hoof scraping wood, rope fibres tightening, one interrupted mouthful, and a distant response would let players piece together what is happening while still outside the room.
99. The horde’s collective voice comes mainly from the front of the procession. This gives the response a direction, but standing alongside thousands of bodies should sound different from listening to them across the valley. Uneven answers travelling along the ranks would communicate their scale.
100. Sound has one on/off control. A player using tablet speakers cannot independently bring up footsteps and threat cues while lowering music. The score also uses low bass pulses whose audibility on your physical speakers has yet to be checked.

101–116. Tablet play, accessibility, saves and performance

101. Touch combat asks too much of the right thumb at once. That thumb normally turns the camera and also activates the sword or potion. A moving wolf can leave the player’s view while the thumb travels to the attack button. The forgiving hit region conceals some of this control problem.
102. The touchscreen layout offers little personal adjustment. Look sensitivity is adjustable. Joystick placement, handedness and action-button positions are fixed. On a larger tablet, where a player grips the edges differently from a phone, that matters.
103. Keyboard-only players cannot freely look up and down. Arrow keys support horizontal turning and movement. Examining an overhead bell, a flying dragon or the floor requires mouse or touch input.
104. The desktop instruction “left click” depends on pointer capture succeeding. With the pointer locked, clicking swings. Without it, clicking the scene tries to acquire mouse look or starts a drag. The F key works, but the headline instruction can give the impression that the sword is unresponsive.
105. Opening the map silently turns off touch sprint. Opening and closing a panel resets movement and the Run toggle. Someone who checks the route during a chase can resume at walking speed without having deliberately changed pace.
106. The potion counter can imply the most important item is missing. Zelda can carry the filled cure while the equipment display reads “Potion · 0,” because it counts only cinder phials. The silver vial deserves a visibly distinct place on his belt or in the HUD.
107. The map has a generic accessible description, but its crucial relationships are painted into a canvas. The player receives some text, including distance, yet a screen reader cannot extract where the moving column lies relative to Zelda, the ford and the gate.
108. Reduced camera motion still permits abrupt compulsory turns. In some ending sequences the smooth turn becomes an immediate change of facing. That removes the sweep but can still leave the player disoriented about what moved and where they are looking.
109. A single saved journey limits comparison between choices. Returning after a completed ending can reopen that ending. The player has no separate save at the witch from which to reconsider the original dilemma while keeping their completed outcome.
110. A failed save is silent. If the browser refuses local storage, the game catches the failure without informing the player. Someone playing a long session can reasonably believe their journey has been preserved.
111. Important observations disappear after a few seconds. There is no lasting record of the shackle, the ribbon, the behaviour of the dead or the warning at the river. Those are precisely the clues a player might want to reconsider after discovering the truth.
112. Automatic graphics reduction only moves downward. The WebGL renderer can lower resolution after a sustained run of slow frames. It does not raise it again when the player leaves the expensive area. A difficult scene can leave a later, simpler scene unnecessarily soft.
113. Very low frame rates slow the game clock as well as the picture. The main loop accepts at most 0.12 seconds per frame. At five displayed frames per second, roughly 0.6 seconds of game time elapses per real second. Severe slowdown changes how the race feels across devices.
114. The software fallback has a measured performance concern. In my 800 × 450 offscreen software-renderer test, eight sampled turning frames in the clearing took about 80–104 milliseconds each. In the hall they took about 104–112 milliseconds each. Those figures are specific to the test environment, but they identify the clearing and hall as priorities for real-device testing.
115. The five passing tests leave important gaps. They establish that the routes and outcomes work under their tested conditions. The movement tests run the world simulation independently, while many tree obstacles are registered by the renderer. Those tests can pass while a visible object in the playable scene is hollow.
116. The game has not earned a claim of maximum Android graphics quality. A proper verdict still needs sustained play on a physical tablet through the clearing, close combat, the densest part of the procession and the hall, with touch responsiveness, frame pacing, image sharpness and speaker intelligibility checked together.

My priority judgment: the largest improvement would come from making three moments trustworthy. At the gate, the game must remember who arrived first. In a fight, Zelda’s blade must hit what the player sees it touch, with a sound that confirms the result. At the banquet, the player should be able to discover what Xero is doing by watching and listening. Those three moments carry the race, the first-person experience and the entire twist.